    $draft$[{"q":"What is the difference between a keyword ability and an ability word?","a":"A keyword ability has rules meaning of its own: the word itself does something, explained by its reminder text. An ability word has no rules meaning; it is an italic label in front of an ordinary ability that groups cards sharing a theme. If removing the word would change what the card does, you have a keyword."},{"q":"How many cards should use a custom keyword?","a":"Enough that players meet it in most games of your set or cube: in a small custom set, often eight to fifteen cards across the rarities and two or more colors. A keyword on two or three cards rarely earns the space; write the ability out in full instead."},{"q":"Should a custom keyword have reminder text?","a":"Yes. Every card with a custom keyword should spell out what it does in italic reminder text, because nobody at the table has read your rules. Printed sets drop reminder text only once a keyword is familiar, and a homemade keyword never is."},{"q":"What makes a keyword parasitic?","a":"A parasitic keyword only works with other cards that carry it or care about it, so it does nothing in an ordinary deck. Mark Rosewater uses the term in his design writing. Open keywords, which work with the creatures, lands and spells players already own, are easier to play and to build around."}]$draft$::jsonb,
